# {{projectName}}

Scaffolded with [`dude`](https://github.com/cubocicloide/dude) using the
`react-fastapi` stack.

---

## Prerequisites

- **Docker Desktop** — running (the whole stack runs in containers).
- **Node.js ≥ 20** and **pnpm** — to provision the project toolchain.

The `dude` CLI and its stack are published on the public npm registry, so no
registry auth or token is needed to install them.

---

## Getting started

The full lifecycle — install the launcher once, scaffold, provision, run:

```bash
# 1. Install the dude launcher globally — the ONLY global install.
#    It auto-runs each project's pinned CLI.
npm install -g @cubocicloide/dude-launcher

# 2. Scaffold the project (this is how {{projectName}} was created).
dude init {{projectName}} --stack react-fastapi
cd {{projectName}}

# 3. Provision the project toolchain — installs the pinned `dude` CLI + stack
#    (and root dev deps) from the lockfile.
pnpm install

# 4. First run — build the images and start every service.
dude up --build
{{#if withPostgres}}

# 5. First run only — apply migrations and seed demo data.
dude reset
{{/if}}

# {{#if withPostgres}}6{{else}}5{{/if}}. From the second run onward, just:
dude up
```

> **Already have the project?** If you're reading this inside `{{projectName}}`
> (or you just `git clone`d it), it's already scaffolded — skip step 2 and start
> at `pnpm install`.
{{#if withPostgres}}

> **Why `dude reset`?** `dude up` applies pending Alembic migrations
> automatically on every start, so the schema is always current — but a fresh
> database has no data in it. `dude reset` drops the database, re-applies
> migrations, and seeds demo users; skip it and pages like **Users** simply
> render empty.
{{/if}}

> **How `dude` resolves its version.** `dude` on your `PATH` is the *launcher*.
> On the first `dude <cmd>` inside a project it provisions the versions pinned in
> `package.json` (running `pnpm install` for you if `node_modules` is missing),
> then re-execs the project-local CLI. So step 3 is optional — `dude up --build`
> would trigger it — but running it explicitly makes the first build faster and
> surfaces any auth problem up front.

